WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.740
The alarm has gone off
at three in the morning.

00:00:03.015 --> 00:00:04.785
Who decides what happens next?

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.130
The first 72 hours go wrong in four ways.

00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:11.353
First, nobody knows who decides.

00:00:11.873 --> 00:00:13.903
It is Sunday, three in the morning,

00:00:14.173 --> 00:00:17.203
and the on-call engineer sees
the encryption spreading.

00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:21.618
Switching off the network would also
stop the factory and the webshop.

00:00:21.928 --> 00:00:24.718
Is that the engineer's call, or the CEO's?

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:27.693
Every minute spent finding out,
the incident grows.

00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:31.533
Second, the evidence is
gone within the hour.

00:00:31.788 --> 00:00:35.138
The instinct is to reboot the
server and wipe the laptop.

00:00:35.608 --> 00:00:38.918
That destroys the memory,
the logs and the timestamps.

00:00:39.158 --> 00:00:43.588
Then we cannot say what was taken,
and the insurer may refuse the claim.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.620
Third, five audiences hear
five different stories.

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:51.435
Staff, customers, press,
the regulator and the insurer.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.110
Someone says no data was
affected before anyone knows.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.040
The legal clocks started
when we became aware:

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.920
a 24-hour early warning
under the NIS2 directive,

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.240
and 72 hours under GDPR.

00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:08.673
Fourth, day one is spent looking for help.

00:01:09.418 --> 00:01:12.908
Forensic firms are fully booked,
because everyone is calling at once.

00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:16.533
Without a retainer,
day one becomes a procurement exercise,

00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:19.023
with the attacker still inside.

00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:21.393
What good looks like.

00:01:21.678 --> 00:01:25.428
A response plan with written
decision rights and named deputies.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.158
A printed phone list.

00:01:27.468 --> 00:01:28.858
Isolate, don't wipe.

00:01:29.228 --> 00:01:31.798
Pre-approved templates,
and a deadline list.

00:01:32.048 --> 00:01:33.918
A retainer signed in peacetime.

00:01:34.188 --> 00:01:37.898
An out-of-band channel,
and a yearly tabletop exercise.

00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:40.943
NIS2 Article 23 sets the clocks.

00:01:42.863 --> 00:01:44.073
A suggested first step.

00:01:44.358 --> 00:01:45.628
Ask two questions.

00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:48.323
When did this board last
rehearse an incident?

00:01:48.598 --> 00:01:51.828
Is the phone list printed somewhere
that is not on the network?

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.340
Nothing on this list is technology.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.240
It is paper,
phone numbers and one rehearsal a year.

